@startuml
title Build abstract seed graph (topology only, no geometry yet)

start
:Scatter seed points inside region\n(blue-noise/Poisson-disc,\ndeterministic from world seed + region coords);
:Add one fixed node per boundary macro-cell\nfrom already-cached neighbor regions\n(pre-colored, single-value domain, not assignable);
:Compute Delaunay triangulation\nover seed points + fixed boundary nodes;
:Take the dual (relaxed Voronoi) -\nedges = "these will touch";

:Seed each unassigned node's domain =\nevery identity compatible with any\nfixed boundary edge already touching it\n(unconstrained nodes start with the full identity set);

if (graph looks colorable in principle?\n(edge density/connectivity roughly matches\nthe compatibility graph's own connectivity -\nsee ARCHITECTURE.md open question)) then (no)
  :Flag graph for reseed;
else (yes)
endif

:Return graph to caller\n(nodes, edges, per-node domain, reseed flag);
stop

note right
  This check is a cheap heuristic, not a proof -
  BiomeGraph's existing eager BFS validation
  (construction-time, whole-graph) already rules out
  "impossible in principle" configs. This step only
  screens for a bad *scatter* (e.g. a node this
  region's scatter isolated from every compatible
  neighbor), not a bad compatibility graph.
end note
@enduml
